        "title": "MSSP's First Public Route: Starting from Open Source",
        "description": "Rather than introducing MSSP only through abstractions, existing projects can become public material for comparison, failure, and reconstruction.",
        "html": "<h1>MSSP&#39;s First Public Route: Starting from Open Source</h1>\n<p>If MSSP remains only an abstract architecture, it can easily become a vocabulary that only its designer understands.</p>\n<p>The first useful public route is therefore not to add more definitions. It is to keep finding open-source projects and identify their state, events, coupling, and replacement boundaries in practice.</p>\n<h2>Comparison matters more than promotion</h2>\n<p>Every reconstruction should preserve at least three views:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a structural map of the original project;</li>\n<li>the MSSP-oriented comparison version;</li>\n<li>failures and counterexamples that resist clean reconstruction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This allows MSSP to face comparisons in implementation, performance, legibility, and maintenance cost instead of merely claiming to look cleaner.</p>\n<h2>Teaching is itself a form of verification</h2>\n<p>If a method can only be operated by its original author, it has not yet become a transmissible method.</p>\n<p>Tutorials, state diagrams, source examples, and version records are therefore not accessories. They are part of whether MSSP can be understood and reused.</p>\n<p>The field lab will begin with small projects, then move toward game runtimes, visual state editors, and continuous operation systems for autonomous agents.</p>\n",
